/**
\page c_style Style Guide: C

\section overview Overview

C is by far the most common language in use at Sun Devil Rocketry. The Avionics
team was founded as a development group for Flight Computers and custom PCBs,
all of which have their firmware written in C. In addition, prior to the formation
of Avionics as an independent group within the club, they were a subteam of Liquids
where a majority of their code was written in C++ (for Arduino), and then C.

As our most common language, we have developed a highly specific code style over time.
This was originally formed by our first avionics lead (Colton Acosta) after doing
an internship at Garmin, and is heavily inspired by that coding style although there
are a few notable differences.

\subsection contact Contact

The authoritative source for style decisions in Sun Devil Rocketry's C code is the
project lead for each project, and then the Avionics Firmware lead, which is currently
[Nicholas Armistead](https://github.com/narmistead).

\section golden_rule SDR Style Guide Golden Rule

All of these rules are general guidance to ensure uniformity and readability in our
code. That goal of making our code readable is the most important thing: if you
need to violate a rule in order to make your code readable, then please do that instead
of conforming blindly to these standards. However, in most scenarios, these rules
should work fine.

\section bracing Indentation/Bracing

We use four spaces per tab for indentation. Ideally, indentation should convert tabs
to spaces, but this has never been enforced and we won't be too picky on it as long
as your editor displays tabs aligned to the next four spaces.

Arguably the most distinctive feature of our coding style is our bracing. We
use a modified variant of [Whitesmiths](https://activeclickweb.com/whitesmiths/index.html)
style indentation, which looks like the following:
\code
if ( foo )
    {
    bar();
    }

foobar();
\endcode
This style places the opening brace on a newline after the condition plus one indentation level.
Statements are placed on the same line as the opening and closing brace. After the closing
brace, de-indent one level. It is strongly encouraged that you include an extra space after the
closing brace as well, although this isn't a hard rule if you have a reason why the next statement
should be grouped with the condition.

\section conditions Conditions

Sun Devil Rocketry has a highly specific style for our conditional statements that
are intended to make long predicates easier to digest.

For simple conditions, the important thing to note is the spaces between if/for/while, 
the opening parentheses, the predicate, and the closing parentheses. For example:
\code
if ( foo )
\endcode

For compound conditions with two components, it is acceptable to place both on the
same line provided the whole condition is visible within the average text editor's 
width (~60 chars). For example:
\code
if ( foo && bar )
\endcode

For compound conditions with three or more components, or two components where the
conditions are too long to include on one line, our rules get a little bit more complex.
An example is provided below:
\code
if ( foo1
  && foo2
  && ( foo3
    || !foo4 )
  || !( foo5 
     && foo6 ) )
\endcode
1. When conditions span multiple lines, the comparison operator (&&, ||) must line up with
the opening parentheses of the previous line or the comparison operator of the prior
condition.

2. When nesting conditions, the whole condition should become indented due to the opening
parentheses and extra whitespace per the prior rules. This enables us to group compound
conditions in a more digestible way.

3. The "not" operator is special since it has only one operand and is half the size of the
other logical operators. For the "not" operator, place this before a condition, even if it 
knocks the condition out of alignment with the previous. The exception is when it is outside
of parentheses -- we would prefer that you align the condition such that rule 1 is preserved.

\subsection demorgan Conditions: De Morgan's Laws

De Morgan's laws are a set of simple rules in formal logic that state:
\verbatim
If not(P and Q), then P or Q.
\endverbatim
as well as its inverse
\verbatim 
If not(P or Q), then P and Q.
\endverbatim

In code, this means:
\code
if !( foo && bar ) ==> if ( foo || bar )
\endcode
and
\code
if !( foo || bar ) ==> if ( foo && bar )
\endcode

You can use these rules to simplify complex logical expressions if needed. Be cautious and
use your best judgement though -- remember that readability is king!

\section functions Functions

Function definitions and forward declarations should be written with the return type 
(and any qualifiers) and the function name on the same line. The opening parentheses
should be on the next line indented one level (like our bracing) with the parameters
listed one-per-line.

\code
static inline float foo
    (
    int a,
    int b
    );
\endcode

In header files, leave one empty line between function declarations. In source files,
leave two empty lines between the last brace of a function and the first line associated
with the next function (including comments).

Functions that do not take any parameters are required to be declared with "void" as the
parameter.

\code
void foo
    (
    void
    );
\endcode

\subsection func_bracing Functions: Indentation & Whitespace

Function bracing varies slightly from our usual bracing style: the opening and closing braces
should not be indented at all, nor should the contents of the base function block.
\code
void foo
    (
    void
    )
{
int a;
int b;
bar(a, b);

} /* foo */
\endcode
Note the comment with the function name after the closing brace.

\section ptrs Pointers

This may not seem like it matters, but we have an opinion about where the
asterisk should be in a pointer definition. At SDR, pointers are considered
their own type, not a modifier of an existing type. As such, we declare our
pointers like this:

\code
int* a;
\endcode

Opinions on this convention differ widely, but we're pretty strongly in this
camp. Arguments for alternative conventions can be found in 
[this Stack Overflow post](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/180401/placement-of-the-asterisk-in-pointer-declarations)
if you're curious. The consensus on our method is that it promotes type safety,
which we see as more important than any argument against the convention.

\section comm Block Comments

Block Comments are comments at the start of a file or preceding a function.
SDR has two conventions for block comments. Doxygen style is our current convention
and is required for new files, but there is an old convention you might find in our
codebase.

\subsection dox Doxygen

Doxygen is a tool that generates documentation from comments in source code. This
guide is written using Doxygen, and it is also used to document a bunch of our
projects including our [mod library](https://docs.sundevilrocketry.org/avionics/mod).

In addition to the ability to generate documentation, Doxygen also plays really nicely
with VSCode, our preferred text editor. C/C++ intellisense can be augmented to pull
from Doxygen comments, which makes writing code easier.

\subsubsection doxfile Doxygen: File Comments

The start of a C source file must have some kind of block comment, and under the Doxygen
conventions this looks like the below:

\verbinclude file_head_dox.c

Each line is preceded by a tag. Each of these has a purpose and is formatted
in a specific way in the generated documentation. 
1. \@file reiterates the file name.
2. \@brief is a short description of what the file does.
3. \@copyright describes the copyright terms of the file. **THIS IS MANDATORY
AT SDR**. Any code that is in an SDR repo is the property of the club. Any 
exceptions should be cleared with the avionics lead. Our default license is the
BSD-3-Clause. Other licenses are acceptable, but we generally prefer permissive
licenses over copyleft licenses like the GPL.
4. \@verbatim formats anything between it and \@endverbatim as a code block in a
monospace font. Some early files that use Doxygen have that tag enclosing
normal documentation or extended descriptions.
5. \@dir indicates that the following documentation applies to the directory that
holds the file. We recommend that this tag be written in the top-level header of
a module.
6. \@section starts a section in documentation. The first argument doesn't really
matter, and the second is used as the section name.
7. \@code and \@endcode enclose monospaced blocks, usually used for code (duh)

\subsubsection doxfunc Doxygen: Function Comments

Functions must be documented as follows in both the source and
header file:
\verbinclude func_def_dox.c

Each line is preceded by a tag. Each of these has a purpose and is formatted
in a specific way in the generated documentation.
1. [required] \@brief is a short description of the function and its purpose.
2. \@note adds a banner with additional important information to the generated
docs.
3. \@warning does the same as the above but with a higher warning level.
4. \@details is a longer description of the function and its purpose.
5. [required] \@param describes a parameter to the function.

The function return MUST be described using one of the following methods:
6. \@return describes what the return of the function represents.
7. \@retval is used for specific return values (i.e. what NAV_BUSY means).
You can have multiple \@retval tags for the same function.

Also note that the doxygen comment has no space between it and the function definition.

\subsection legacy Legacy Comments

Our legacy comment format is a lot simpler and looks like the below:

File:
\verbatim
/*******************************************************************************
*
* FILE: 
* 		ignition.c
*
* DESCRIPTION: 
* 		Contains API function to the engine controller ignition system and 
*       contintuity readings
*
* COPYRIGHT:                                                                   
*       Copyright (c) 2025 Sun Devil Rocketry.                                 
*       All rights reserved.                                                   
*                                                                              
*       This software is licensed under terms that can be found in the LICENSE 
*       file in the root directory of this software component.                 
*       If no LICENSE file comes with this software, it is covered under the   
*       BSD-3-Clause.                                                          
*                                                                              
*       https://opensource.org/license/bsd-3-clause          
*
*******************************************************************************/
\endverbatim

Note that the legacy style still notes copyright information -- this is required
no matter what!

Function:

\verbatim
/*******************************************************************************
*                                                                              *
* PROCEDURE:                                                                   * 
* 		ign_cmd_execute                                                        *
*                                                                              *
* DESCRIPTION:                                                                 * 
* 		Executes an ignition subcommand based on user input from the sdec      *
*       terminal                                                               *
*                                                                              *
*******************************************************************************/
\endverbatim

These comments are only required above the actual implementation, headers do not
have these in the legacy style.
*/